//? Title: Convex Hull: Two Chains, Exact Turns and Actual Stack Edits
//? Subtitle: Andrew's monotone chain with original identities, degeneracy controls and the original Gaussian cloud
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a finite set of planar points, return the extreme vertices of the smallest
convex region containing them, in boundary order. Sorting and local turn tests
make the construction simple, but a rounded determinant can erase a genuine
corner. This report follows Andrew's two monotone chains from actual native
stack events, checks duplicates and collinear boundary points, and reproduces
the original square and forty-point cloud. The canonical hull now uses an exact
orientation sign for the stored binary64 coordinates, with bounded integer
arithmetic rather than a floating tolerance. Actual work counters separate the
sorting cost from the linear scans.
)

\toc

> The method: sort once, scan twice

The convex hull is the set of convex combinations of the input points. In two
dimensions it is a polygon, segment, singleton or empty set. The API returns its
extreme vertices: counterclockwise from the lexicographically smallest (x,y),
without repeating the first vertex. Collinear edge interiors are omitted from
the vertex list but still belong to the closed hull region. This is the API's
strict-vertex convention, not a claim that every input lies strictly inside.

After exact-coordinate deduplication, sort by x and then y. Build a lower chain
left to right and an upper chain right to left. Before pushing candidate c,
inspect the top two stack points a,b using

\[
D(a,b,c)=(b_x-a_x)(c_y-a_y)-(b_y-a_y)(c_x-a_x).
\]
\alttext(The turn determinant is the horizontal first displacement times vertical second displacement minus the vertical first displacement times horizontal second displacement.)

A positive sign is a left turn. A negative sign is a right turn and zero is
collinear. While the sign is nonpositive, pop b and test again; then push c.
Join the chains after dropping each chain's last endpoint, which is shared with
the other. The exact predicate returns only the sign, not a determinant magnitude.

> A right-turn removal and the actual completed chains

Use the sorted points v0=(0,0), v1=(0,2), v2=(1,1), v3=(2,0), v4=(2,2).
The lower scan's first test is D(v0,v1,v2)=−2, so v1 is removed from that chain.
It remains a vertex of the upper boundary. The centre v2 is later removed from
both chains. The first panel draws the actual recorded test; the second draws
the producing kernel's completed lower and upper stacks.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hull_chains.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::geometry::convex_hull_trace({{0,0},{0,2},{1,1},{2,0},{2,2}});
  const auto at=std::find_if(t.events.begin(),t.events.end(),[](const auto& e){
    return e.pass==dm::geometry::HullPass::lower && e.action==dm::geometry::HullAction::test;
  });
  const auto a=t.sorted[at->a].point,b=t.sorted[at->b].point,c=t.sorted[at->candidate].point;
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(110,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke old;old.color={148,163,184};old.width=1.5;old.arrow=true;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke blue;blue.color={37,99,235};blue.width=2.2;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke red;red.color={220,38,38};red.width=2.2;
  d.path({{a.x,a.y},{b.x,b.y},{c.x,c.y}},old)
   .path({{a.x,a.y},{c.x,c.y}},blue)
   .ellipse({a.x,a.y},.05,.05,{15,23,42},blue)
   .ellipse({b.x,b.y},.06,.06,{220,38,38},red)
   .ellipse({c.x,c.y},.05,.05,{37,99,235},blue)
   .text({a.x-.3,a.y-.3},"a",18).text({b.x-.55,b.y+.35},"b: pop",18)
   .text({c.x+.35,c.y},"c",18)
   .text({.5,3.2},"First lower test: right turn",20)
   .text({.5,-.9},std::format("exact sign {}; remove b",at->turn),18);
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<t.sorted.size();++i) {
    const auto p=t.sorted[i].point;
    d.ellipse({5.8+p.x,p.y},.045,.045,{100,116,139},old)
     .text({5.8+p.x+(p.x==0?-.25:p.x==2?.25:0),p.y+(p.y==0?-.28:.3)},std::format("v{}",i),17);
  }
  std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> lower,upper;
  for(auto i:t.lower) lower.push_back({5.8+t.sorted[i].point.x,t.sorted[i].point.y});
  for(auto i:t.upper) upper.push_back({5.8+t.sorted[i].point.x,t.sorted[i].point.y});
  d.path(lower,blue).path(upper,red)
   .text({6.8,3.2},"Completed actual chains",20)
   .text({6.8,-.9},"blue lower; red upper",18).save_svg("build/hull_chains.svg");
  const std::array<std::string_view,3> names{"test","pop","push"};
  std::print("| lower event | action | candidate | edited point | tested triple | exact sign | stack size after |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: |\n");
  std::size_t k=0;
  for(const auto& e:t.events) if(e.pass==dm::geometry::HullPass::lower) {
    const bool test=e.action==dm::geometry::HullAction::test;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",k++,names[std::size_t(e.action)],e.candidate,e.point,test?std::format("({}, {}, {})",e.a,e.b,e.candidate):"none",test?std::to_string(e.turn):"none",e.stack_size);
  }
  std::print("\n{} exact tests, {} pushes and {} pops across both passes. {} retained hull vertices.\n",t.analysis.work.orientation_tests,t.analysis.work.pushes,t.analysis.work.pops,t.analysis.hull.size());
}
