\image(figures/hull_chains.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The first lower test removes an upper-boundary point from the lower stack. Completed chains, read directly from the native trace, omit the interior centre and join into the square.)
\alttext(On the left a grey path through a at the origin, b at zero comma two and c at one comma one is replaced by a blue direct link from a to c. On the right blue lower and red upper chains enclose a square around a grey centre.)

Why do local pops suffice? With points processed in lexicographic order, a
non-left turn puts b on or above the shortcut from a to c, so b cannot be a
necessary vertex of the current lower convex envelope. Popping until all retained
turns are left turns restores that envelope invariant. Induction over the sorted
points gives the full lower boundary; reversing the order gives the upper one.
Their union is convex and encloses every input point. Every retained corner is an
input extreme point, so the polygon is the smallest enclosing convex region.
The same nonpositive rule retains only extreme endpoints of a collinear chain.

> Duplicates and lower-dimensional hulls

Empty input returns no vertices. One distinct point returns that point. Two
points return sorted endpoints, and any larger collinear set returns its two
extremes. The vertex list is unclosed: a segment has two entries, not an artificial
three-point polygon. Duplicate coordinates, including signed zeros, keep the
earliest matching original identity and its coordinate representation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hull_degeneracies.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::string,std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D>>> cases{
    {"empty",{}},{"one distinct",{{2,3},{2,3}}},{"two unsorted",{{3,0},{0,0}}},
    {"collinear with duplicate",{{0,0},{1,0},{2,0},{3,0},{1,0}}},
    {"edge and interior points",{{6,0},{8,0},{8,2},{6,2},{7,0},{8,1},{7,1},{6,0}}}
  };
  std::print("| case | records | distinct | hull vertices | earliest original indices in hull order |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& [name,p]:cases) {
    const auto a=dm::geometry::convex_hull_analyze(p);
    std::string ids;for(auto i:a.input_indices) ids+=std::format("{}{}",ids.empty()?"":", ",i);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",name,p.size(),a.work.distinct_points,a.hull.size(),ids.empty()?"none":ids);
  }
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(110,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke blue;blue.color={37,99,235};blue.width=2;
  for(std::size_t index:{3,4}) {
    const auto& p=cases[index].second;const auto h=dm::geometry::convex_hull(p);
    std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> outline;
    for(const auto& v:h) outline.push_back({v.x,v.y});
    if(h.size()==2) d.path(outline,blue);else d.polygon(outline,{37,99,235,.06},blue);
    for(const auto& v:p) d.ellipse({v.x,v.y},.045,.045,{100,116,139},blue);
    for(const auto& v:h) d.ellipse({v.x,v.y},.065,.065,{220,38,38},blue);
  }
  d.text({1.5,2.8},"Collinear: two extremes",20)
   .text({7,2.8},"Edges: corners only",20)
   .text({4,-1.1},"Omitted vertices still belong to the closed hull region",19)
   .save_svg("build/hull_degeneracies.svg");
}
