\caption(The original seeded forty-point cloud has eight hull vertices on this toolchain. Native equal coordinate scales preserve its geometry; the table rounds coordinates for display while the hull uses their full stored values.)
\alttext(A blue polygon encloses forty grey standard-normal samples and marks its eight extreme vertices in red. The polygon is not closed by a duplicated output record.)

The entire sampled stream and hull agree with the former report's generator and
algorithm on this build. A seed does not guarantee identical normal variates
across C++ standard libraries. The coordinates are unsnapped; exact orientation
classifies the stored double values without pretending they are unrounded real
observations. No external dataset is needed to check these geometric examples.

> Checking the linear scans and sorting cost

Let n be the original count, m the distinct count and h the hull size. Sorting
costs O(n log n) comparisons, then deduplication is linear. For m≥2 each of the
two separate scans pushes every distinct point once. A point can be popped at
most once per pass. The final chain lengths add to h+2, so the exact stack identity is

\[
\mathrm{pushes}=2m,\qquad \mathrm{pops}=2m-h-2.
\]
\alttext(For at least two distinct points the two scans perform twice m pushes and twice m minus h minus two pops.)

Every nonpositive orientation test causes a pop. Each candidate causes at most
one final positive test, so fewer than 4m orientation tests suffice across both
scans. Compact test/pop/push events therefore use O(m) storage; copying every
full intermediate stack would instead risk quadratic trace storage.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hull_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> sizes,normal_tests,parabola_tests,bound;
  std::print("| cloud | original n | distinct m | hull h | sort comparisons | exact tests | pushes | pops | peak chain |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(int n:{16,32,64,128,256}) {
    const auto gaussian=dm::geometry::convex_hull_analyze(dm::geometry::sample_gaussian_points(n));
    const auto parabola=dm::geometry::convex_hull_analyze(dm::geometry::random_point_cloud(n,dm::geometry::PointCloud::Parabola,17));
    for(const auto& [name,a]:std::array<std::pair<std::string_view,dm::geometry::ConvexHullAnalysis>,2>{{{"normal",gaussian},{"parabola",parabola}}}) {
      const auto& w=a.work;
      std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",name,n,w.distinct_points,a.hull.size(),w.sort_comparisons,w.orientation_tests,w.pushes,w.pops,w.max_stack);
      if(w.pushes!=2*w.distinct_points || w.pops!=2*w.distinct_points-a.hull.size()-2 || w.orientation_tests>=4*w.distinct_points) return 1;
    }
    if(parabola.hull.size()!=std::size_t(n)) return 1;
    sizes.push_back(n);normal_tests.push_back(double(gaussian.work.orientation_tests)/gaussian.work.distinct_points);
    parabola_tests.push_back(double(parabola.work.orientation_tests)/parabola.work.distinct_points);bound.push_back(4);
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(sizes,normal_tests,"normal",{37,99,235},2).points(sizes,normal_tests,"",{37,99,235},5)
      .line(sizes,parabola_tests,"parabola",{220,38,38},2).points(sizes,parabola_tests,"",{220,38,38},5)
      .line(sizes,bound,"bound 4m",{100,116,139},1)
      .legend({"normal","parabola","bound 4m"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38},{100,116,139}})
      .title("Actual exact-sign tests stay below four per distinct point")
      .x_label("original point count n").y_label("orientation tests / distinct count m")
      .y_limits(0,5).size(900,560).save_svg("build/hull_work.svg");
}
