\caption(Both the small-hull normal samples and all-extreme parabola samples satisfy the actual push/pop identity and test bound. Comparison counts include the producing standard-library sort; the figure measures scan work, not wall-clock speed.)
\alttext(Blue normal-cloud and red parabola-cloud exact tests per distinct point stay below a grey line at four. All parabola points are extreme, while only some normal samples are hull vertices.)

The overall upper bound is O(n log n) comparison work plus linear exact-sign
calls, with O(n) scratch/output storage. Binary64 inputs have a fixed word size,
so the bounded integer predicate contributes a fixed arithmetic cost per call.
Sorting can be reduced to worst-case hull construction by placing distinct keys
on y=x² and reading their lower boundary in order. This motivates the familiar
comparison-model worst-case lower bound, not a claim that every small-hull input
requires that much work or that comparison sorting is optimal in every word-RAM
model. Output-sensitive alternatives can exploit small h.

> Exact signs and numerical limits

A finite binary64 coordinate is a signed integer mantissa times a power of two.
Expand the determinant into six coordinate products, grouping positive and
negative terms. Scaling them all by 2^2148 makes them integers. Each scaled
product needs at most 4196 bits; even six same-sign terms need at most 4199
bits. Two 132-word arrays, each holding 4224 bits, therefore suffice to sum and
compare the two sides without floating multiplication, overflow or underflow.
This is a fixed dyadic-integer implementation, not Shewchuk's adaptive expansion
algorithm. The cited robustness work motivates the distinction between a rounded
determinant and its exact sign.

For a visible cancellation control, set M=2^52, a=(0,0), b=(M,M−1), c=(M+1,M).
The exact determinant is M²−(M−1)(M+1)=1, although separately rounded double
products can cancel to zero. All three points must remain hull vertices.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  using P=dm::geometry::Point2D;
  const double m=std::ldexp(1.,52),tiny=std::numeric_limits<double>::denorm_min(),huge=std::numeric_limits<double>::max();
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::string,std::array<P,3>>> cases{
    {"unit left turn",{{{0,0},{1,0},{0,1}}}},
    {"cancellation determinant 1",{{{0,0},{m,m-1},{m+1,m}}}},
    {"subnormal triangle",{{{0,0},{tiny,0},{0,tiny}}}},
    {"huge collinear triple",{{{-huge,-huge},{huge,huge},{0,0}}}}
  };
  std::print("| represented triple | old double determinant | exact sign |\n| :--- | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& [name,p]:cases) {
    const auto approximate=dm::geometry::cross(p[0],p[1],p[2]);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",name,std::isfinite(approximate)?std::format("{:.3e}",approximate):"nonfinite",dm::geometry::orientation_sign(p[0],p[1],p[2]));
  }
  std::print("\nCancellation control hull vertices: {}.\n",dm::geometry::convex_hull({{0,0},{m,m-1},{m+1,m}}).size());
  const auto exact=dm::geometry::convex_hull_trace({{0,0},{1,0}},{2,4});
  std::print("Two-point trace uses {} actual events.\n",exact.events.size());
  try { (void)dm::geometry::convex_hull_trace({{0,0},{1,0}},{2,3});return 1; }
  catch(const std::length_error&) {std::print("One-below actual event cap is rejected.\n");}
  try { (void)dm::geometry::convex_hull_trace({{0,0},{1,1},{std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN(),2}},{1,1});return 1; }
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {std::print("All coordinates validate before point/event caps, including a late NaN.\n");}
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| represented triple | old double determinant | exact sign |
| :--- | :--- | ---: |
| unit left turn | 1.000e+00 | 1 |
| cancellation determinant 1 | 0.000e+00 | 1 |
| subnormal triangle | 0.000e+00 | 1 |
| huge collinear triple | nonfinite | 0 |

Cancellation control hull vertices: 3.
Two-point trace uses 4 actual events.
One-below actual event cap is rejected.
All coordinates validate before point/event caps, including a late NaN.
// result_end

Exactness applies to the coordinates actually stored in Point2D. It cannot recover
information lost before conversion to double or account for measurement error.
Nonfinite coordinates are rejected before internal indexed copies, sorting or
optional complete-work caps. The legacy by-value call still performs its argument
copy at the call boundary. Positive max_points counts original records, including
duplicates; max_events counts actual compact records online. Zero means unlimited,
and analysis ignores the optional event cap.

The original cross(), squared_distance() and distance() primitives retain their
double arithmetic and documented range limitations. This change makes the
canonical monotone-chain hull and orientation_sign exact in their combinatorial
turn decisions; it does not upgrade every other geometry algorithm or make SVG
coordinates exact at arbitrary extreme scales. The largest cancellation example
is shown numerically because its half-unit triangle area is not visible at that
coordinate scale in a normal floating drawing.

Independent native checks enumerate all 512 subsets of a three-by-three grid,
6,561 ordered four-record grid tuples and 1,000 seeded integer clouds against a
supporting-edge oracle. Actual edits, source identities and scan counts are
checked separately. A repeatable optional CTest oracle uses Python's standard
Fraction arithmetic for 20,000 raw-binary64 or near-collinear triples, including
one-working-ULP perturbations; it requires no numerical package or binding.

> Conclusion

Two exact-sign chain scans return the extreme vertices and retain the original
square and seeded-cloud results. Actual stack records expose why points disappear
from one chain, while degeneracy controls distinguish omitted vertices from
points outside the hull. The numerical controls demonstrate why a double-valued
cross product alone does not establish robustness. Bounded exact dyadic arithmetic
supplies that sign guarantee for finite binary64 input, with explicit identities,
limits and measured work.

> References

- A. M. Andrew (1979), *Another efficient algorithm for convex hulls in two dimensions*, Information Processing Letters 9(5), 216–219. [Publisher record](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0020019079900723), [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(79)90072-3).
- J. R. Shewchuk (1997), *Adaptive Precision Floating-Point Arithmetic and Fast Robust Geometric Predicates*, Discrete & Computational Geometry 18(3), 305–363. [Author's paper](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~jrs/papers/robustr.pdf). The current predicate uses fixed integer sums rather than that paper's adaptive implementation.
- [C++ normal_distribution specification](https://eel.is/c++draft/rand.dist.norm.normal), for the original standard-normal distribution interface. Preserving engine, seed and call order reproduces this build, without promising portable normal variates across standard libraries.
